  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1211 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nA break from the monotonous, but very pleasant, weather conditions\nwill unfold late Friday into Saturday as a vort lobe separates\nfrom a parent low over the northeastern Pacific. PWATs may rise to\n0.7 to 0.8 inch over the southern border of our CWA by late Friday\nafternoon, but this will not be a robust subtropical tap of\nmoisture as the negatively-tilted low crosses the upper Gulf of CA.\nInstability will also be lacking with LI's hovering around 0 C \nand minimal CAPE, so precipitation that would favor Catron county \nand our southwestern zones would mostly consist of primarily showers\nlate Friday afternoon before struggling to spread northeastward \ninto Friday night. Otherwise, temperatures would cool a couple of \ndegrees in response to lowering pressure heights Friday afternoon,\nand southwestern zones would run a couple degrees below normal \ndue to increasing cloud cover.\n\nThe low would weaken into a shortwave as it crosses NM Friday\nnight into Saturday morning with a separate trough having moved\ninland off of the eastern Pacific and poised to cross near the\nFour Corners through the day Saturday. This will introduce a\ndistinct new area of precipitation, driven by modest baroclinicity\nand associated upper dynamics that would favor the northern half\nof NM. Temperatures would cool 5 to 10 degrees below normal,\nenough to lower snow levels to 9,000 to 10,000 ft through the day\nSaturday, setting up the northern mountains for a few inches (1 to\n6 inches with localized peaks exceeding) through Saturday night \nas snow would transition toward the Sangre de Cristos with snow \nlevels lowering closer to 7,500 to 8,500 feet. Rainfall amounts in\nsurrounding northern and central zones would tend to range from a\nfew hundredths of an inch to a half inch Saturday and Saturday \nnight. \n\nThe trough will move into TX, briefly closing into a low on\nSunday. Some lingering showers may impact the far northeastern\ncorner of NM, but otherwise drier and cool (readings near to 10\ndegrees below normal) will prevail in the more subsident\nnorthwesterly flow aloft. Surface winds are modeled to turn a bit\nstronger in the plains with a northerly to easterly wind direction\non the backside of the departing trough/low.\n\nA transient ridge will then cross NM through early to midday\nMonday with dry conditions and light to moderate southerly breezes\nprevailing. Uncertainties begin to arise into next Tuesday and\nWednesday as another deeper northeastern Pacific trough moves\ninland. The deterministic GFS solution and ensemble mean from the\nGEFS are lagging behind the more progressive ECMWF/ENS with the\narrival of this trough/low. The latter solutions would\nconsequently introduce rain and high elevation snow earlier on\nTuesday rather than Wednesday as the former solutions indicate.\nNeither suite of solutions indicates any tap of subtropical\nmoisture, but do bring in a maritime polar airmass with sufficient\nforcing and ascent for another round of rain and mountain snow \nfor western and central NM zones.",
